SHE'S BACK -- TONYA GLIDES AGAIN

WEST VIRGINIA -- She may have cried, stumbled and fallen on her butt
twice, but Tonya Harding insists she's thrilled with her pro debut and
comeback into the strange world of competitive figure skating.

"Sure, sure the jumps weren't that great," the trailer park tigress
admitted, "But you try sitting around eating chips and watching Springer
all day long and see how great your triple toe loop is."

Harding says she's anxious to start pulling in some 'real' money after
being blacklisted from the skating world for the past five years.  She
says she survived by selling her story to tabloid TV shows, coaching
part time and doing the occasional mud-wrestling appearance.

It was all smiles and kisses as the other skaters hired to ensure
Harding's comeback was not a complete debacle stood by her.  The group
of aging has-beens gave her a group hug and some were even overheard
thanking her for 'smacking that toothy bitch Nancy.'

Although crowds of figure skating fans failed to materialize, the event
was swarmed by many national and international networks eager to catch a
possible rink-side tantrum, bludgeoning or cat fight.

"What a bloody waste of time," groused one BBC cameraman.  "I came three
thousand miles to film a poxy spiral and camel spin.  We was promised a
real story!  No one honestly gives a crap about the wholesome fresh
start for Tonya angle."

